{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And the Egyptians heard, etc.</b> One needs to know why it says <b>וְשָׁמְעוּ</b> with the addition of a vav at the beginning; it should have said ״יִשְׁמְעוּ מִצְרַיִם.״ One further needs to know the intent of his saying <b>כִּי הֶעֱלִיתָ</b> — if his intention is to say that the Egyptians will hear about this exodus, that is baffling, for did they not see the deeds of HaShem and the wonders He performed among them? And further, what does their being killed have to do with the Egyptians' hearing that He brought them out by His strength, etc.? And if the intention is to identify which Egyptians those who will hear are — meaning, those whom You brought out by Your strength, etc. — this is difficult: are there other Egyptians to be mistaken for? And even if there were other Egyptians, in any case the plain reference is only to the known Egyptians." "<b>And it appears</b> that the intention concerns what we shall raise against Moses, peace be upon him: why did he not mention the arguments he presented before HaShem in the matter of the calf? And although I explained in the verse ״עַד אָנָה יְנַאֲצֻנִי״ etc. that HaShem concealed this argument with the argument of ״נִלְאֵיתִי נְשׂוֹא,״ and also because this provocation has no limit — with all this, Moses should have mentioned the merit of the fathers together with the arguments he mentioned here, by which he prevailed over the judgment, as I shall explain." "However, Moses, peace be upon him, was wise in the pleasantness of his words, for he said ״וְשָׁמְעוּ״ with the addition of a vav — to say: besides the earlier arguments that he made when he prayed over the matter of the calf, which he now repeats and argues and adds to them in order to respond to HaShem's pronouncement. And that is his saying <b>וְשָׁמְעוּ מִצְרַיִם</b> etc. And he gave a reason for his words, stating the matter with certainty that they will hear. And if you should say: who told you that they will surely hear — perhaps they will not hear? For this he said <b>כִּי הֶעֱלִיתָ בְּכֹחֲךָ הַגָּדוֹל</b> etc. And he was precise in saying <b>מִקִּרְבּוֹ</b> — to say that although Egypt's trials were known to all, nevertheless the matter will be different for those who were in their midst, and a nation such as that will not refrain from investigating what is done with Israel and their affairs. Therefore I declare with certainty that they will know what will be done to them precisely. According to this, the word ״כִּי״ gives the reason for the statement ״וְשָׁמְעוּ.״" "<b>Further,</b> he intends along this line: that he comes to give a reason why he was concerned only with the Egyptians' hearing and drawing the consequence from it, and not from the other nations. For this he said <b>כִּי הֶעֱלִיתָ בְּכֹחֲךָ</b> etc. — meaning: they are the ones who knew that this nation has no ability of its own and could not have come up from Egypt except by Your great strength. When they hear that they died as one — even though HaShem will fulfill His promise and bring Moses's offspring into the land — nevertheless they will posit that that particular family had the strength to withstand the inhabitants of the land. And this is in accordance with what is customary, that a strong nation rules over one weaker than itself; but the people who came out of Egypt, who had nothing but the strength of HaShem — there would not be, heaven forbid, strength in HaShem to bring them in. And this is what he was precise in saying: <b>הֶעֱלִיתָ בְּכֹחֲךָ אֶת הָעָם הַזֶּה</b> — pointing with a finger at the people who came out, who had no strength apart from supernal strength — to disqualify any nation coming in their place that has no miracle to refute the argument of ״the inability of HaShem.״ And for this reason the concern is only from the side of Egypt; but as for the other nations, were it not for the reason that Egypt knew the people and their nature, there would have been no profanation of the Name. You therefore find two things entailed from Moses's words: first, that the Egyptians will hear what is done with Israel; and second, that the Egyptians knew that Israel have no strength to prevail in great wars apart from the strength of the Blessed One."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {3 "The phrase חִלּוּל הַשֵּׁם (profanation of the Name) is written in the source with a holam/waw variant; translated according to standard reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:07.619929842Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}